# 2. GOVERNING PRINCIPLES

These requirements are mandatory.

## 2.1 Foundation first — no existing implementation

The coding agent MUST work from the Readiness R0 foundation (repository skeleton, pinned versions, environments). There is no existing repository implementation to inspect — no prototype exists (confirmed 2026-11-20).

It must not assume:

- A prototype, implementation, or production data already exists.
- The current architecture is incorrect.
- Database tables, once built from Product P1 onward, can be replaced without an approved migration.
- Authentication, once built, can be discarded.
- Components need to be rewritten without evidence.
- Features are incomplete without testing them.
- User data, once it exists, is disposable.

Working functionality, once built from Product P1 onward, must be preserved unless a documented defect or approved improvement requires a change.

## 2.2 No invented functionality

The Kea Parrot Master PRD (KEA-PRD-001) remains the source of truth for product functionality.

The agent MUST NOT independently introduce:

- New product features.
- New user roles.
- New business models.
- New payment systems.
- New database entities without justification.
- Unapproved external services.
- Unapproved architecture changes.
- Unapproved user permissions.
- Unapproved data collection.

If an implementation detail is missing, the agent must document the ambiguity and propose a decision before proceeding.

## 2.3 No cosmetic completion

A button is not complete because it looks clickable.

A page is not complete because it renders successfully.

A feature is not complete because it works with hardcoded data.

A real-time feature is not complete because it displays a simulated notification.

A form is not complete because it accepts input.

Completion requires a verified end-to-end workflow, including the frontend, backend, database, security controls, error handling and tests.

## 2.4 No destructive shortcuts

The agent MUST NOT:

- Drop production tables.
- Reset production databases.
- Delete user records.
- Replace authentication identifiers.
- Remove database policies without reviewing their effects.
- Disable security policies to make features work.
- Replace real backend functionality with mock data.
- Bypass failing tests by removing the tests.
- Hide errors instead of fixing their causes.

All potentially destructive changes require a documented impact assessment, backup or recovery strategy, and explicit approval.

