# 6. READINESS R3 — SECURITY AND PRIVACY HARDENING

**Priority: Critical — Release Blocking**

Conduct a comprehensive security review.

## 6.1 Authentication security

Verify:

- Secure session handling.
- Token validation.
- Session revocation.
- Password reset security.
- Email verification.
- Protection against account enumeration where appropriate.
- OAuth callback validation, if enabled.
- Protection against session fixation.
- Appropriate cookie settings.

## 6.2 Authorisation security

Test:

- Horizontal privilege escalation.
- Vertical privilege escalation.
- Access to another user's resources.
- Moderator privilege boundaries.
- Administrator privilege boundaries.
- Super-administrator protection.
- Unauthorised role changes.
- Direct API access that bypasses UI restrictions.

The backend must enforce all permissions.

## 6.3 Application vulnerability testing

Assess:

- Cross-site scripting (XSS).
- SQL injection.
- Cross-site request forgery (CSRF), where applicable.
- Broken access control.
- Insecure direct object references.
- Server-side request forgery (SSRF), where applicable.
- Open redirects.
- File upload abuse.
- Dependency vulnerabilities.
- Secret exposure.
- Rate-limit bypass.
- Brute-force attacks.
- Spam and automated abuse.
- Insecure error messages.

Use established security testing tools and manual review where appropriate.

Do not claim that the application is penetration-tested unless actual testing has been conducted.

## 6.4 Privacy controls

Verify:

- Collection minimisation.
- User consent records.
- Account data access.
- Data correction.
- Account deletion.
- Data retention rules.
- Anonymous content privacy.
- Location privacy.
- Notification preferences.
- Administrative access restrictions.
- Audit logging.

Exact GPS coordinates must never be exposed in public content or profiles.

Location-based discovery must use the approved approximate-location model.

Do not introduce background location tracking.

## 6.5 Moderation and abuse prevention

Verify:

- Reporting functionality.
- Report persistence.
- Report status management.
- Moderator access controls.
- Content removal.
- User restrictions.
- Account suspension.
- Blocking.
- Moderation audit logs.
- Enforcement consistency.
- Appropriate user feedback.
- Incident escalation.

Moderation actions must be auditable.

Privileged users must not be able to silently alter or erase moderation history.

## 6.6 Compliance readiness

Assess readiness for:

- Ghana Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843).
- Applicable Data Protection Commission requirements.
- Relevant privacy and consumer protection obligations in future markets.
- Appropriate terms of service.
- Privacy policy.
- Cookie and tracking disclosures where applicable.
- Age eligibility (16+) and consent requirements. Users aged 16–17 are included in the 16+ audience; there is no adult-only content in the MVP; Ghana Data Protection Act 2012 (Act 843) / DPC review is still required before public launch. Parental-consent mechanism: none defined — legal-review item (TBD, owner Dennis, before public launch).

Obtain qualified legal review where necessary.

Do not describe the application as legally compliant merely because technical controls have been implemented.

