# 16. CHANGE CONTROL AND AI CODING AGENT GOVERNANCE

These instructions govern every implementation session.

## 16.1 Before each Readiness phase (R0–R10)

Produce a Readiness phase execution plan containing:

- Objectives.
- Requirement IDs.
- Existing implementation affected.
- Files and modules affected.
- Database objects affected.
- API and integration changes.
- Security implications.
- Dependencies.
- Tests required.
- Migration requirements.
- Backup requirements.
- Rollback or recovery approach.
- Acceptance criteria.

## 16.2 During implementation

The agent MUST:

- Work incrementally.
- Preserve existing functionality.
- Follow existing conventions unless a change is approved.
- Use shared components where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary duplication.
- Validate all server-side inputs.
- Enforce server-side permissions.
- Write or update tests.
- Keep migrations version-controlled.
- Report unexpected discoveries.
- Stop when a change creates an unapproved architectural or data-integrity risk.

## 16.3 Git discipline

Before risky work:

- Check Git status.
- Identify uncommitted user changes.
- Do not overwrite unrelated work.
- Create an appropriate checkpoint after confirming the working tree is safe.

After completing each Readiness phase:

- Review the diff.
- Run relevant tests.
- Record changes.
- Commit using a descriptive message where authorised.
- Preserve a clear rollback point.

Never use destructive Git operations to clean up the repository without explicit approval.

## 16.4 Readiness phase completion report

Every Readiness phase (R0–R10) must end with a formal report:

1. Readiness phase (R0–R10) and name.
2. Objectives completed.
3. Requirement IDs addressed.
4. Files created.
5. Files modified.
6. Files removed and justification.
7. Database changes.
8. API changes.
9. Security changes.
10. Tests executed.
11. Test results.
12. Defects discovered.
13. Outstanding issues.
14. Risks.
15. Deployment implications.
16. Git commit or checkpoint reference.
17. Exit-gate assessment.

The agent must distinguish between completed, partially completed, blocked and unverified work.

## 16.5 Mandatory approval points

Obtain explicit approval before:

- Replacing the existing architecture.
- Introducing a new infrastructure service.
- Changing authentication architecture.
- Changing user roles or permissions.
- Performing destructive database operations.
- Removing existing product functionality.
- Changing the approved product scope.
- Running risky production migrations.
- Deleting production records.
- Changing billing or payment functionality.
- Launching public production.

